        "| Metric       | Mean Speed Increase | Standard Deviation |\n",
        "|--------------|---------------------|--------------------|\n",
        "| Inner Join        | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_join:.2f}\"`   | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_std_join:.2f}\"`    |\n",
        "| Union All  | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_union:.2f}\"` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_std_union:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| Filter  | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_filter:.2f}\"` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_std_filter:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip collapse=\"false\" title=\"AI Generated Summary\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "**Joins (Inner + Union All):** Polars performs joins faster than pandas by using parallelized hash join algorithms implemented in Rust and operating directly on Arrow memory, minimizing copying and serialization overhead.  \n",
        "\n",
        "**Filter Clauses:** Polars evaluates filter expressions lazily and in parallel at the columnar level, enabling vectorized, cache-efficient filtering that significantly outpaces pandas’ row-wise execution in Python.\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Conclusion\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "Across the board `Polars` is faster at all of the typical dataframe operations we tested. The syntax differences are minimal and AI can quickly convert your `Pandas` code. Make this transition and speed up your work!\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "# Code References\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: { style=\"margin-top: -15px; font-size: 90%;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.table-responsive}\n",
        "| Operation | **Pandas Example** | **Polars Example** | **Times Faster** |\n",
        "|------------|--------------------|--------------------|-----------------------|\n",
        "| **Read CSV** | `df = pd.read_csv(\"data.csv\")` | `df = pl.read_csv(\"data.csv\")` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| **Write CSV** | `df.to_csv(\"out.csv\")` | `df.write_csv(\"out.csv\")` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_write:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| **Constructor** | `df = pd.DataFrame(data)` | `df = pl.DataFrame(data)` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_constructor:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| **Group By** | `df.groupby(\"col\")[\"value\"].mean()` | `df.groupby(\"col\").agg(pl.col(\"value\").mean())` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_groupby:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| **Inner Join** | `df1.merge(df2, on=\"id\", how=\"inner\")` | `df1.join(df2, on=\"id\", how=\"inner\")` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_join:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| **Union All** | `pd.concat([df1, df2], ignore_index=True)` | `df1.vstack(df2)` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_union:.2f}\"` |\n",
        "| **Filter** | `df[df[\"value\"] > 10]` | `df.filter(pl.col(\"value\") > 10)` | `{python} f\"{speed_increase_mean_filter:.2f}\"` |\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "*** _Results will vary depending on machine and size/structure of your datasets._\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip collapse=\"true\" title=\"**AI Generated Summary**: Why Union All Has High Variance \"}\n",
        "\n",
        "Polars’ `pl.concat()` (or `vstack()`) uses **multi-threaded Arrow buffer operations** that cut average runtime but introduce **non-deterministic variance**. On macOS with M1 Max chips, the OS may schedule threads differently across performance and efficiency cores, causing some runs to finish faster or slower. In short, **parallelism improves mean speed but naturally increases variance**—explaining the wider spread in the “Union All” benchmark.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-tip collapse=\"true\" title=\"**AI Generated Summary**: Why Constructor is Reliably Faster \"}\n",
        "\n",
        "The constructor benchmark is the most reliable because it’s a **pure in-memory, CPU-bound operation** with no I/O, threading, or garbage collection noise. Each run follows the same predictable memory layout, making it a clean measure of Polars’ and Pandas’ internal efficiency without interference from system variability.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n"
